from common import Batch
b=Batch('NEXT',1)
def q(*args,**kw): b.add(*args,**kw)
q('M','Components','Server default','In the App Router, what is the default component category for layouts and pages?','Server Components.','Layouts and pages are Server Components by default. Browser interaction belongs behind an appropriate Client Component boundary; a .tsx extension does not imply client execution.','Look at the App Router default.','Is a client directive present?','next:app/getting-started/server-and-client-components',wrong=['Client Components in every case.','Web Workers.','Pages Router components.'])
q('C','Components','Client boundary','What does "use client" establish in a module?','It marks a boundary into the client module graph. The component and its client dependencies can use client capabilities. It does not mean the initial HTML is never rendered on the server.','A Client Component can contribute server-rendered initial HTML and later hydrate. The directive is about component/module capability and bundling, not exclusively where every render happens.','Separate the module boundary from initial HTML.','Can client code still be prerendered?','next:app/api-reference/directives/use-client')
q('B','Components','State in Server Component','Why does this App Router page fail, and what is the narrowest useful restructuring?','useState requires a Client Component. Move Counter into a separate module starting with "use client" and render it from the Server page.','The page is server by default.~The state Hook requires client execution.~A small client boundary preserves the surrounding server-rendered structure instead of moving the whole page unnecessarily.','Identify the interactive subtree.','Which component actually needs state?','next:app/getting-started/server-and-client-components#reducing-js-bundle-size','import { useState } from "react";\n\nexport default function Page() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>{count}</button>;\n}')
q('C','Routing','page and layout','How do page.js and layout.js differ in a nested App Router route?','A page provides the route’s unique UI. A layout wraps its segment and descendants with shared UI. Shared layouts preserve state across compatible client navigations.','A layout is not rerendered from scratch for every sibling navigation in the way a remounted wrapper would be. Route structure determines which layouts are shared.','Separate shared shell from leaf content.','Which UI should survive navigation?','next:app/getting-started/layouts-and-pages')
q('W','Routing','Root layout','Write the minimal app/layout.jsx that accepts children and supplies an English root document.','export default function RootLayout({ children }) {\n  return (\n    <html lang="en">\n      <body>{children}</body>\n    </html>\n  );\n}','The root layout creates the document structure.~children contains the active route subtree.~The root must include html and body; set metadata through the Metadata API rather than manually duplicating head management.','The root owns the document shell.','Which two elements are required?','next:app/api-reference/file-conventions/layout#root-layout','// Implement app/layout.jsx.')
q('M','Navigation','Link component','Which API is the standard declarative choice for internal App Router navigation?','Link from next/link.','Link integrates client navigation and prefetch behavior. Raw anchors still navigate but do not provide the same framework navigation integration by default.','Use framework-aware navigation.','Which component represents a route link?','next:app/api-reference/components/link',wrong=['A global window.nextRouter object.','getInitialProps.','A script tag with a route URL.'])
q('W','Route handlers','JSON response','Write app/api/health/route.js returning { ok: true } from GET.','export async function GET() {\n  return Response.json({ ok: true });\n}\n','The route file exports the HTTP method handler.~Response.json serializes the object with an appropriate JSON content type.~No React component is returned; unsupported methods are handled according to route-handler rules.','A Route Handler returns a Response.','Name the export after the HTTP method.','next:app/api-reference/file-conventions/route','// Implement app/api/health/route.js.')
q('C','Loading','loading.js boundary','What does a loading.js file provide for a route segment?','It defines an immediate loading UI. Next.js wraps the segment’s page and children in a Suspense boundary using that fallback. This enables streaming while slower content becomes ready.','The loading UI does not make every nested request parallel automatically. Work outside the boundary can still delay reaching it.','Think about an automatic boundary.','Which subtree can stream after the fallback?','next:app/api-reference/file-conventions/loading')
q('B','Routing','Async params','Correct this Next.js 16 dynamic page’s access to params.','params is a Promise. Use: export default async function Page({ params }) { const { slug } = await params; return <h1>{slug}</h1>; }','The framework supplies asynchronous route params.~Await resolves the segment values.~Reading params.slug synchronously relies on an obsolete access pattern.','Request APIs became asynchronous.','Resolve the params value before destructuring.','next:app/api-reference/file-conventions/page#params-optional','export default function Page({ params }) {\n  return <h1>{params.slug}</h1>;\n}')
q('M','Assets','Public paths','A file is stored at public/logo.svg. Which src path references it?','/logo.svg','The public directory maps files from the site root. Including public in the URL adds a path segment that is not part of that mapping.','Public is a filesystem convention.','What is the served URL root?','next:app/api-reference/file-conventions/public-folder',wrong=['/public/logo.svg','/app/public/logo.svg','public://logo.svg'])
q('C','Environment','Public variables','What does NEXT_PUBLIC_ mean for an environment variable used in client code?','It allows the value to be exposed to the browser bundle. Next.js inlines eligible references at build time. Treat it as public information, not as a place for secrets.','Changing deployment environment values after building does not rewrite an already inlined bundle. Server-only secrets should remain outside client-exposed variables.','Consider who can inspect the bundle.','When is the value embedded?','next:app/guides/environment-variables#bundling-environment-variables-for-the-browser')
q('W','Metadata','Static metadata','Write app/about/page.jsx with the title About and description About our team using the Metadata API.','export const metadata = {\n  title: "About",\n  description: "About our team"\n};\n\nexport default function AboutPage() {\n  return <h1>About</h1>;\n}\n','The server page exports a static metadata object.~Next.js creates the relevant head metadata.~This export belongs in a Server Component module, not a module marked use client.','Metadata is a route export.','Keep the page on the server.','next:app/api-reference/functions/generate-metadata#the-metadata-object','// Implement app/about/page.jsx.')
q('S','Assets','Image dimensions','Why supply width and height for a remote next/image image, or use a correctly sized fill container?','They establish the image’s aspect ratio or layout space. Reserving space reduces layout shifts while it loads. CSS can still control the displayed size.','Intrinsic dimensions are not a command to render at that exact CSS size in every layout. A fill image needs a positioned, dimensioned parent.','Reserve geometry before bytes arrive.','What determines the aspect ratio?','next:app/api-reference/components/image#width-and-height')
q('C','Fonts','next/font purpose','What does next/font improve compared with adding an arbitrary remote font stylesheet at runtime?','It integrates font loading and optimization into the application build. Supported fonts can be self-hosted with the deployment. It helps control loading behavior and layout stability through generated font styles.','Using a font utility does not eliminate every possible layout shift. Choose appropriate weights, subsets and fallback behavior.','Think about build-time asset preparation.','Does the browser need a separate third-party stylesheet request?','next:app/getting-started/fonts')
q('M','Directives','use server meaning','What does "use server" mark when used at the top of an async function?','A Server Function callable through the supported server invocation mechanism.','It marks a server-executed function, not a general Server Component declaration. Server Components are already the default in the App Router.','The directive applies to functions.','Is this a component-category switch?','next:app/api-reference/directives/use-server',wrong=['Every nested component as a Client Component.','A synchronous browser-only function.','A guarantee that callers are authenticated.'])
q('U','Components','Browser-only globals','Follow-up to NEXT-002: is it safe to read window during a Client Component’s initial render?','Not generally, because its initial HTML can be rendered on the server. Read browser-only APIs in an appropriate client lifecycle or event handler. Use an explicitly client-only loading strategy when a library requires it.','The client directive does not create window in the server runtime. A typeof guard that changes initial markup can also cause hydration mismatches.','Client capability does not remove prerendering.','Where is window guaranteed to exist?','next:app/getting-started/server-and-client-components#on-the-client-first-load')
q('B','Request APIs','Async cookies','Correct this current App Router cookie read.','Await the cookie store: const token = (await cookies()).get("token")?.value;','cookies returns a Promise.~Awaiting yields the store used for get.~Cookie access is request-specific; do not place it directly inside a shared use cache scope.','Resolve the request API.','What does cookies return now?','next:app/api-reference/functions/cookies','import { cookies } from "next/headers";\n\nconst token = cookies().get("token")?.value;')
q('C','Actions','Authorization in actions','Why must a Server Action validate input and authorization even when its form is hidden from unauthorized users?','The action is a server entry point. UI visibility is not an authorization boundary. Validate the caller and resource permissions inside the action before mutating data.','Client-controlled arguments and hidden inputs are untrusted. A server directive changes execution location, not the trustworthiness of a request.','Assume callers can bypass the UI.','Where does the mutation enforce permission?','next:app/guides/data-security#built-in-server-actions-security-features')
q('B','Actions','Redirect catch','Why can this try/catch swallow a successful redirect, and where should redirect move?','redirect throws framework control flow. Call redirect("/done") after the try/catch that handles save failures.','save completes successfully.~redirect throws its navigation signal.~The broad catch mistakes that signal for a save error. Keep redirect outside the handled error region.','Navigation can use control flow exceptions.','Which statement should not be caught as a save failure?','next:app/api-reference/functions/redirect#behavior','try {\n  await save();\n  redirect("/done");\n} catch (error) {\n  return { error: "Save failed" };\n}')
q('W','Route handlers','Query parsing','Write a GET Route Handler returning the q query parameter, defaulting only when it is absent.','export async function GET(request) {\n  const url = new URL(request.url);\n  const query = url.searchParams.get("q") ?? "";\n  return Response.json({ query });\n}\n','Construct a URL from the request URL.~get returns the first matching value or null when absent.~The nullish default preserves an explicitly empty q. Repeated parameters need getAll if the contract accepts them.','Use the Web URL API.','What sentinel means the parameter is absent?','next:app/api-reference/file-conventions/route#url-query-parameters','// Implement app/api/search/route.js.')
q('M','Routing','Route groups','What does a folder named (marketing) do to the URL path?','It groups routes without adding that folder name to the URL.','Route groups organize layouts and routes. They do not create a literal parenthesized URL segment; conflicting resolved paths remain invalid.','Parentheses mark organization.','Does this folder contribute a segment?','next:app/api-reference/file-conventions/route-groups',wrong=['It adds /(marketing) to every nested URL.','It makes every nested route dynamic.','It automatically protects nested routes with authentication.'])
q('S','Layouts','Templates remounting','When is template.js more appropriate than a shared layout.js?','Use a template when navigation should recreate the wrapped subtree. Its state and Effects can reset with the new instance. Use a layout for shared UI that should preserve state across compatible navigation.','Templates and layouts have different identity behavior. A template is not simply a differently named layout with identical persistence.','Ask whether state should survive navigation.','Do Effects need a new instance lifecycle?','next:app/api-reference/file-conventions/template')
q('C','Rendering','Static and request-time work','How do prerendered content and request-time content differ in the App Router?','Prerendered content can be prepared before a particular request. Request-time content depends on information available for that request. Cache Components can combine a reusable shell with streamed dynamic regions.','The distinction is not identical to Server versus Client Components. A Server Component can participate in either static preparation or request-time rendering.','Separate component location from rendering time.','Does the result need request-specific data?','next:app/getting-started/caching')
q('M','Caching','Uncached fetch baseline','With Cache Components disabled in Next.js 16, which fetch option explicitly bypasses persistent data caching?','{ cache: "no-store" }','no-store requests fresh data for that fetch. Browser caching and Next.js server data caching are distinct contexts; this question concerns the server fetch extension.','Specify the server caching policy.','Which option opts out?','next:app/api-reference/functions/fetch',wrong=['{ cache: "force-cache" }','{ next: { revalidate: 3600 } }','{ next: { revalidate: false } }'])
q('W','Caching','Time-based fetch revalidation','With Cache Components disabled, write getCatalog() fetching https://example.com/catalog.json with a 300-second revalidation interval and checking HTTP success.','export async function getCatalog() {\n  const response = await fetch("https://example.com/catalog.json", {\n    next: { revalidate: 300 }\n  });\n  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\n  return response.json();\n}\n','The extended fetch options set the data revalidation interval.~A non-2xx response is rejected explicitly.~The policy is demand-driven revalidation, not a timer that guarantees a new request exactly every 300 seconds.','Use the server fetch extension.','Check response.ok before decoding.','next:app/api-reference/functions/fetch#optionsnextrevalidate','// Assume the endpoint returns JSON when successful.\n// Cache Components is disabled. Implement getCatalog().')
q('C','Caching','Request memoization scope','With Cache Components disabled, how does render-time GET fetch memoization differ from the persistent Data Cache?','Request memoization deduplicates matching work during a React server render. The Data Cache can reuse cached results across requests according to policy. Memoization alone does not promise persistence across requests.','The scopes and lifetimes differ. Route Handlers are outside the React component tree, so do not assume the same render memoization there.','Compare one render with many requests.','Which layer outlives a render pass?','next:app/deep-dive/caching#request-memoization')
q('S','Caching','Full Route Cache versus Router Cache','With Cache Components disabled, distinguish the Full Route Cache from the client Router Cache.','The Full Route Cache stores server-rendered route output. The Router Cache stores route payload segments in the browser for navigation. Invalidating one layer does not mean every unrelated cache layer is automatically cleared.','A stale navigation can involve client-held data even after server data changes. Diagnose which layer served the observed result before choosing invalidation.','Server and browser hold different artifacts.','Which cache is local to a tab session?','next:app/deep-dive/caching#client-side-router-cache')
q('B','Caching','Refresh is not invalidation','With Cache Components disabled, why can router.refresh still show an old cached server response?','refresh requests a new server payload but does not invalidate the Data Cache. Invalidate the relevant server cache with revalidatePath or revalidateTag as appropriate.','The client refresh clears/reloads the route view.~The server can satisfy its work from a still-valid cache.~Refreshing transport and invalidating data are separate operations.','Trace where the old value lives.','Did the server cache entry change?','next:app/api-reference/functions/use-router#userouter','router.refresh();\n// The server still uses a valid cached product fetch.')
q('W','Caching','Tag stale-while-revalidate','Write an async Server Action invalidateCatalog that marks tag catalog stale using the recommended stale-while-revalidate profile. Assume authorization already completed in its caller for this exercise.','"use server";\n\nimport { revalidateTag } from "next/cache";\n\nexport async function invalidateCatalog() {\n  revalidateTag("catalog", "max");\n}\n','The action executes on the server.~The max profile marks tagged entries stale for stale-while-revalidate behavior.~It does not eagerly fetch every entry immediately, and tags must already be attached to cached data.','Choose the revalidation profile explicitly.','Which profile uses stale-while-revalidate?','next:app/api-reference/functions/revalidateTag','// Implement app/actions.js.\n// This isolated exercise assumes authorization is already enforced.')
q('M','Caching','Read your own writes','Inside a Server Action, which API immediately expires tagged data so the next read waits for fresh data?','updateTag(tag)','updateTag supports read-your-own-writes and is limited to Server Actions. revalidateTag(tag, "max") uses stale-while-revalidate instead.','The user must see the just-saved value.','Which tag API has immediate-expiry semantics?','next:app/api-reference/functions/updateTag',wrong=['revalidateTag(tag, "max")','router.prefetch(tag)','cacheTag(tag)'])
q('C','Caching','Tag assignment','What is the difference between cacheTag and revalidateTag?','cacheTag associates labels with cached work. revalidateTag invalidates data identified by a label. Tagging alone neither performs a mutation nor forces a refresh.','Invalidation requires a matching tag on the cached entry. A tag string is not automatically inferred from a URL or database table name.','One labels; one invalidates.','Was the entry actually tagged?','next:app/api-reference/functions/cacheTag')
q('B','Caching','Request data inside cache','With Cache Components enabled, what is wrong with reading cookies inside this use cache function?','Request-specific APIs cannot be read directly in this shared cached scope. Read the cookie outside and pass the required value as an argument.','The cache needs explicit inputs.~cookies depends on the current request.~Moving the read outside makes the relevant argument part of cache identity; authorization still belongs at the correct boundary.','Make request dependencies explicit.','Can a shared cache safely read hidden per-request input?','next:app/api-reference/directives/use-cache#request-time-apis','async function getTheme() {\n  "use cache";\n  return (await cookies()).get("theme")?.value;\n}')
q('W','Caching','Cached pure computation','With Cache Components enabled, write async formatLabel(name) using use cache and the hours cacheLife profile. Return the trimmed uppercase name.','import { cacheLife } from "next/cache";\n\nexport async function formatLabel(name) {\n  "use cache";\n  cacheLife("hours");\n  return name.trim().toUpperCase();\n}\n','The directive makes the async function cacheable.~Its serializable name argument contributes to cache identity.~cacheLife sets an explicit lifetime profile. This tiny calculation is illustrative; real caching should justify its overhead.','Put the directive inside the function.','Where must cacheLife be called?','next:app/api-reference/functions/cacheLife','// name is a string. Cache Components is enabled.\n// Implement formatLabel(name).')
q('S','Metadata','Dynamic metadata','How should a product page derive its title from route parameters and server data?','Export an async generateMetadata function from a Server page or layout. Await params and fetch the needed data there. Return a metadata object with the derived title and description.','Do not export both metadata and generateMetadata from the same segment. Client-only document.title effects do not replace the server metadata contract.','Metadata has its own server entry point.','Which export supports asynchronous derivation?','next:app/api-reference/functions/generate-metadata#generatemetadata-function')
q('M','Images','Remote allowlist','Which next/image setting narrowly controls approved remote image URL patterns?','images.remotePatterns','Remote patterns constrain allowed protocols, hosts and paths. Broad arbitrary remote access can create unwanted image-optimization exposure.','Think about an explicit allowlist.','Which image config matches URLs?','next:app/api-reference/components/image#remotepatterns',wrong=['images.allowEveryHost','metadata.images','experimental.anyRemoteImage'])
q('B','Images','Responsive sizes','Why can a responsive fill image download an unnecessarily large resource when sizes is missing?','The browser may assume a 100vw slot. Add a sizes value matching the layout, such as sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 50vw" for that two-width layout.','The generated srcset offers candidate widths.~sizes describes the expected rendered slot.~An inaccurate slot estimate makes resource selection inefficient. CSS still defines the actual layout.','The browser needs a slot-size estimate.','Does the image always occupy the full viewport?','next:app/api-reference/components/image#sizes','<div style={{ position: "relative", height: 300 }}>\n  <Image src="/hero.jpg" alt="Mountain" fill />\n</div>')
q('C','Proxy','Middleware migration','What is the current Next.js 16 convention for code historically placed in middleware.ts?','Use proxy.ts and export a proxy function. The middleware file convention is deprecated. Keep request interception focused and enforce data authorization again where protected data is accessed.','Renaming reflects the current API direction. Proxy can redirect or rewrite requests, but matching a route is not a complete authorization design.','Check the current file convention.','Which name replaced middleware?','next:app/api-reference/file-conventions/proxy')
q('W','Proxy','Targeted redirect','Write proxy.js redirecting /legacy and its subpaths to /docs using a matcher.','import { NextResponse } from "next/server";\n\nexport function proxy(request) {\n  return NextResponse.redirect(new URL("/docs", request.url));\n}\n\nexport const config = {\n  matcher: "/legacy/:path*"\n};\n','The matcher limits invocation to the legacy route family.~The handler builds an absolute destination from the request origin.~The redirect changes the browser URL; rewrite would preserve the visible URL.','Constrain interception before running it.','Which response changes the address bar?','next:app/api-reference/file-conventions/proxy#matcher','// Implement proxy.js at the project root.')
q('C','Routing','Redirect versus rewrite','When should a rewrite be used instead of a redirect?','Use a rewrite to serve another destination while keeping the visible request URL. Use a redirect when the client should navigate to a different URL. Consider canonical URLs and caching according to the intended public route.','A rewrite is not a client navigation. Returning a redirect status instructs the client to make a different request.','Separate served destination from visible URL.','Should the address bar change?','next:app/api-reference/functions/next-response#rewrite')
q('B','Navigation','App Router imports','Correct this router import for an App Router Client Component.','Use: import { useRouter } from "next/navigation";','next/router belongs to the Pages Router.~App Router client navigation uses next/navigation.~Pathname and search params have separate Hooks rather than the old router object shape.','Router families have different APIs.','Which package serves the App Router?','next:app/api-reference/functions/use-router#migrating-from-nextrouter','"use client";\nimport { useRouter } from "next/router";')
q('S','Search params','Server search parameters','For an App Router page that must use a query filter on the server, how should it read searchParams?','Accept the page’s searchParams prop and await it. Validate the resulting string or string-array values. Keep request-dependent work inside the appropriate dynamic boundary when Cache Components is enabled.','The page prop is not URLSearchParams and is asynchronous in current Next.js. A layout is not the right place to assume fresh page query props.','Use the page request input.','Is the prop a Promise or a URLSearchParams instance?','next:app/api-reference/file-conventions/page#searchparams-optional')
q('M','Route handlers','Body consumption','Which expression reads a JSON request body in an async Route Handler?','await request.json()','The Web Request body is consumed asynchronously. Read it once or clone appropriately before consumption; merely reading a body property does not decode JSON.','Request follows the Web API.','Which method performs JSON decoding?','next:app/api-reference/file-conventions/route#request-body',wrong=['request.body.json','JSON.parse(request.body)','request.query.json()'])
q('S','Security','Server-only modules','How can a data-access module prevent accidental import into a Client Component module graph?','Add import "server-only" to the module. Next.js can then report an invalid client import. Keep secrets and privileged operations behind that server boundary.','Naming a file server.js alone is not the same enforcement mechanism. Public environment variables remain public regardless of the module’s intent.','Use a checked boundary marker.','Can the build catch environment poisoning?','next:app/getting-started/server-and-client-components#preventing-environment-poisoning')
q('B','Hydration','Time-dependent rendering','Why can this page hydrate inconsistently, and how should the initial timestamp be handled?','Server and client can compute different timestamps. Pass a stable server-produced value for the initial render, or render a matching placeholder and update after hydration.','Server HTML contains one time.~The client’s initial render can contain another.~The initial trees disagree; suppressHydrationWarning hides a narrow warning rather than fixing general mismatches.','Compare server and client execution times.','Can both initial renders use one value?','next:messages/react-hydration-error','export default function Clock() {\n  return <time>{new Date().toISOString()}</time>;\n}')
q('U','Caching','Path versus tag invalidation','Follow-up to NEXT-031: when is a tag preferable to a path for invalidating a shared product record?','Use a tag when that record feeds several routes. The shared tag identifies the data dependency across those routes. Path invalidation targets a route rather than automatically expressing every consumer of that record.','Choose invalidation according to dependency scope. Revalidating one path does not inherently refresh all other pages sharing tagged data.','Follow the data’s consumers.','Is the change tied to one route or one entity?','next:app/api-reference/functions/revalidatePath#relationship-with-revalidatetag-and-updatetag')
q('A','Caching','Cache Components shell','With Cache Components enabled, design a product page with cached public details and request-specific account pricing.','Keep reusable public details in an explicit cached scope. Read account information outside shared cached scopes and render request-specific work under an appropriate Suspense boundary. Make any user-dependent cache keys and authorization rules explicit.','Caching a whole mixed page under an incomplete key risks cross-user data reuse. A reusable shell and streamed personalized region have different lifetimes.','Separate public and personalized dependencies.','Which value makes one user’s result different?','next:app/getting-started/caching')
q('C','Caching','cacheLife dimensions','What do stale, revalidate and expire describe in a cacheLife profile?','stale controls client reuse before checking with the server. revalidate controls when server entries can refresh in the background. expire controls when an entry is too old to serve without waiting for fresh work.','These fields govern different stages. Treating every number as a single hard TTL obscures stale-while-revalidate behavior.','There is more than one freshness boundary.','Which one forces waiting for fresh work?','next:app/api-reference/functions/cacheLife#reference')
q('B','Caching','updateTag context restriction','Why is updateTag invalid here, and which current API fits this webhook’s stale-while-revalidate requirement?','updateTag is limited to Server Actions. Use: revalidateTag("catalog", "max"); after authenticating the webhook.','This function is a Route Handler, not a Server Action.~The updateTag context restriction applies.~revalidateTag supports handlers and an explicit profile; invalidation endpoints must validate callers.','Check the execution context.','Which invalidation API supports Route Handlers?','next:app/api-reference/functions/updateTag#usage','import { updateTag } from "next/cache";\n\nexport async function POST() {\n  updateTag("catalog");\n  return Response.json({ ok: true });\n}')
q('C','Parallel routes','Slots and URLs','How do parallel route slots such as @analytics relate to URLs and layouts?','Slots let a layout render multiple route regions in parallel. Their names do not become URL segments. The layout receives each slot as a prop and controls its placement.','A slot is not a route group or ordinary path segment. Active slot state during soft navigation can differ from what can be recovered on a full reload.','A layout can have multiple route outlets.','Does the @ folder add to the path?','next:app/api-reference/file-conventions/parallel-routes')
q('S','Parallel routes','default.js fallback','Why might a parallel route need default.js even though soft navigation appears to work?','A hard reload may not determine the active state for an unmatched slot. default.js supplies a fallback for that case. Without an appropriate fallback, the unmatched slot can lead to a 404.','Soft navigation retains slot state in ways a fresh request cannot reconstruct. Testing only in-app navigation misses that difference.','Compare retained state with a fresh request.','What should an unmatched slot show on reload?','next:app/api-reference/file-conventions/parallel-routes#defaultjs')
